{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Take yourself a calf, a young bull, for a sin-offering.</b> Some raise the difficulty: why did Aharon's calf come as a sin-offering while Israel's calf came as a burnt-offering? And they said concerning this: because in the sin of the calf Aharon did not sin in thought — for surely he did not intend it for idolatry at all — and the essence of his sin was in the deed, in that he actually made the calf, as it is written (Exodus 32:35): ״וַיִּגֹּף ה׳ אֶת הָעָם עַל אֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ אֶת הָעֵגֶל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה אַהֲרֹן״ — what does it teach by saying ״אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה אַהֲרֹן״? Rather, it comes to teach that Aharon sinned only in the making of the calf and not in thought; therefore his offering is a calf for a sin-offering, which atones for the sin of deed. But Israel, who sinned also in thought — for surely they intended it for idolatry — therefore their offering is a calf for a burnt-offering, which pertains to the sin of imagination. And what is difficult about this explanation: did Israel not also sin in deed, for they bowed down to it and sacrificed to it? Why did they not bring a calf for a sin-offering to atone? It appears to me that for the sin of slaughter they brought the goat as atonement, because it is said (Leviticus 17:7): ״וְלֹא יִזְבְּחוּ עוֹד אֶת זִבְחֵיהֶם לַשְּׂעִירִים״ — and where do we find that Israel slaughtered to the satyrs, that it says ״עוֹד״ ('anymore')? Surely it refers to what is said of the calf: ״וַיִּזְבְּחוּ לוֹ״ — for this slaughter was surely to the satyrs, to the demons who are no god, for that entire deed was a deed of Satan, and satyrs dance there (Isaiah 13:21). Therefore it says: 'Take a goat for a sin-offering.' But Aharon, who did not slaughter to it at all, brought a calf for a sin-offering, to atone for what he did with the calf." "And as for what is written concerning Aharon's offering, ״וְהַקְרֵב לִפְנֵי ה׳״, whereas concerning the people's offering it says ״לִזְבֹּחַ לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — some say it is because slaughter is valid when performed by non-priests, but the act of bringing near is not. And it appears to me that it is because the ultimate purpose of the sacrifices of Elohim is to bring a person to a broken spirit, and Israel sinned also in the spirit of imagination; therefore concerning them it says ״לִזְבֹּחַ לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — that after slaughter they would be לִפְנֵי ה׳. But Aharon, who did not sin in thought — therefore immediately after the taking of the calf for a sin-offering it says ״וְהַקְרֵב לִפְנֵי ה׳״, for immediately and at once he becomes near to HaShem after the act of taking."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:23.925282280Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}